    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fault planes, stratigraphic layers, and stratigraphic features interpreted within a 3D volume of seismic reflection data collected over the Elk Hills Oil Field are integrated with numerical models to develop a fault evolution history that is structurally, stratigraphically, and mechanically consistent. The interpretation of growth structures visible within the seismic data, as well as isochores constructed from stratigraphic interpretations, constrain the chronology of faulting within the field and bracket fault initiations between stratigraphic horizons. Numerical models verify that the interpreted fault geometry is mechanically sound and place additional constraints on fault evolution in terms of relative amount of slip on each fault. The results of this study suggest that during the time period investigated, mid Miocene to present, the eastern and the western parts of the Elk Hills Anticline have been developing in response to slip along faults within two distinct fault systems. In the east, the east-west trend of the faults is characteristic of a relic structural fabric. In the west, this older structural fabric has been overprinted by a northeast vergent thrust system consisting of northwest-southeast striking thrust sheets. These two systems converge along the axis where the strike of the anticline changes from northwest-southeast to east-west. This finding is consistent with the tectonic interpretation of the western part of Elk Hills as a thrust related structure.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
